.docname {Playback and rules}
.include {docs}

## Playback

| Action               | Key               | Button   |
|----------------------|-------------------|----------|
| Run or pause         | .keybinding {Space} | **Play** / **Pause** |
| Advance one generation | .keybinding {J} | **Step** |
| Go back to the start of the run | .keybinding {B} | **Reset to start** |
| Reset the generation counter to 0 | .keybinding {0} | **Reset generation counter** in Settings |

Resetting the generation counter leaves the cells as they are.

### Reset to start

**Reset to start** returns to the board as it was before its first generation since your latest edit, including the generation counter, and pauses. Use it to rerun a pattern, or to change it and try again.

Pausing and stepping stay part of the same run. Drawing, erasing, stamping, randomizing, clearing, or changing the rule moves the start to the edited board, even while the simulation runs, so a reset never discards an edit. Undo, redo, and resizing the board end the run instead, and the button stays disabled until the next generation. With [Fill canvas](settings.qd#fill-canvas) on, resizing the window resizes the board too.

A reset is itself an [undoable edit](editing.qd#undo-and-redo): undo returns to where the run had got to, and redo resets again.

### When the board settles

Sooner or later most boards stop evolving: everything left is still, or repeats. The status bar notices as soon as a generation repeats one from the last 128, and shows it in place of the population change: `stable` for a board that no longer changes, and `period 2` for one that repeats every two generations. Hover over it to see the generation the cycle began at. The label stays until something changes how the board evolves: an edit, undo, a board resize, or switching edges.

Turn on **Pause when settled** in [Settings](settings.qd#simulation) to stop there. Playback pauses at the first repeat with a short message saying why, which helps at ∞ speed. It pauses only once, so pressing Play continues past it.

128 generations is enough for mixes of the common oscillators, even though their periods combine. Longer cycles are not noticed. A glider circling a wrapped board is the usual one, since it returns to the same cell only after a very long time. Gliders on a [fixed](#edges) board crash into an edge instead.

> Tip: .keybinding {Space} keeps playing and pausing after you click a toolbar button. Only a button you reach with .keybinding {Tab} takes .keybinding {Space} for itself. Click the board, or press .keybinding {Tab} until the board is outlined, to give .keybinding {Space} back to playback.

## Speed

Speed is shown in generations per second in the toolbar's speed field, for example `40 gen/s`. It ranges from 1 to 40 gen/s, or **∞**, maximum speed. A green fill behind the number shows how far along that range the speed is, up to the **∞** toggle at the field's right end, which it reaches at 40 gen/s.

- Click **∞** to switch maximum speed on. The toggle lights up and the fill fills the field. Click it again to return to the speed you had before.
- Click **‹** or **›** to change the speed by 1 gen/s, within 1 to 40. They appear while you hover over the field, or when you reach them with .keybinding {Tab}. **‹** at ∞ returns to 40.
- Drag sideways on the field to scrub through the speeds; hold .keybinding {Shift} for finer control. A drag stays within 1 to 40; one that starts at ∞ leaves it as soon as it goes left, and comes back to ∞ if it returns past 40. .keybinding {Esc} during a drag restores the previous speed.
- Click the field to type a speed from 1 to 40, decimals included, or `∞`. Since ∞ is hard to type, `max` and `inf` work too. The unit is hidden while you type, and typing it, as in `20 gen/s`, is fine too. .keybinding {Enter} applies it, and .keybinding {Esc} cancels.
- Press .keybinding {plus} to speed up and .keybinding {minus} to slow down while the board has keyboard focus. Each press changes the time between generations by 25 ms. .keybinding {plus} at 40 gen/s also goes to ∞, and .keybinding {minus} at ∞ returns to 40.

### Max speed

At ∞, each frame runs as many generations as fit in about 8 ms, up to 1024, and leaves the rest of the frame for drawing and input. While it runs, the field shows the rate actually achieved instead of ∞, which depends on the board size and how busy it is; paused, it shows ∞ again. Editing the field starts from ∞ either way.

### Catching up after a stall

At a fixed speed, a frame that arrives late runs the generations it missed, up to 8 at once. If it falls further behind, for example while you drag the window, the backlog is dropped rather than carried over, so the app never freezes trying to catch up.

## Rules

A rule says how many live neighbours make a dead cell come alive (*birth*, **B**) and let a live cell stay alive (*survival*, **S**). Conway's Game of Life is `B3/S23`.

Choose a rule in **Settings**, either from a preset or by typing a rulestring and clicking **Apply** beside it. The preset matching the active board rule is highlighted, including after an import or undo; a custom rule leaves all presets unselected.

| Preset          | Rulestring       | Character                                                           |
|-----------------|------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Life**        | `B3/S23`         | Conway's original                                                   |
| **HighLife**    | `B36/S23`        | Like Life, plus a small *replicator* pattern                        |
| **Day & Night** | `B3678/S34678`   | Live and dead cells behave symmetrically                            |
| **Seeds**       | `B2/S`           | No cell survives; patterns explode                                  |

### Rulestring formats

Any Life-like rule works. These are all accepted, ignoring case and spaces around the slash:

| Form               | Example     | Meaning                           |
|--------------------|-------------|-----------------------------------|
| `B…/S…`            | `B36/S23`   | Birth on 3 or 6, survive on 2 or 3 |
| `S…/B…`            | `S23/B36`   | The same rule, survival first     |
| `…/…` (legacy)     | `23/36`     | Survival counts first, then birth |

Each count is a digit from 0 to 8. Either side may be empty, as in `B2/S`. The rule is always displayed in `B/S` form.

Invalid rulestrings are rejected with a message below the field, and the current rule stays in place.

### Rules and undo

Changing the rule is an undoable edit. Undo brings back the previous rule together with the board as it was. Importing an RLE pattern that declares a different rule also changes the board rule, and is undone the same way (see [Patterns and RLE](patterns.qd#importing-a-pattern)).

## Edges

The board is finite, so its edges need a rule of their own. Press .keybinding {M}, click the edges button in the status bar, or choose under **Edges** in **Settings** to switch between the two modes. The status bar button shows the current one, and appears once the window is at least 850 pixels wide.

| Mode      | Behaviour                                                                                  |
|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Wrap**  | Opposite edges are joined, like a torus. A glider leaving on the right comes back on the left. This is the default. |
| **Fixed** | Everything outside the board counts as dead. A pale wall marks the edge.                   |

The edge mode is saved between runs. Unlike the rule, switching edges is not an edit, so undo does not change it.
